> pages, even telnet services could all be aggregated, according to
> a user-centric topic criterion, and linked from one web page.  This
> is why the web became the first port of call and thus took over
> pretty much the whole internet.

Gopher had some advantages (more structure, easy navigation) over the
WWW but in the end the more free form WWW won out.

> Usenet held on for a long time, because newsreaders are
> very featureful, but make no mistake:  eventually the web
> will completely displace it.  It's just taking a lot longer.
> [...]

What you say is true but IMO is not the my main criticism with web
forums.

Usenet has a protocol and a (more or less) standardized format for the
message body and the meta data. So you can choose different clients
for accessing them (like for example you can choose different browsers
for browsing the net).

Web forums have RSS now but the messages are still heavily customized
HTML. Every forum looks different and has different means of
scoring/filtering/threading ... if they have that at all. Most don't.

Sure, sometimes progress has to go back one step so it can take two
steps further later. But web forums seem to me to have taken several
steps back just to get HTML (what Usenet is capable of displaying,
too, only most newsreader don't support them well) while neglecting
all other cool features from Usenet and even don't seem to be evolving
at all.

